# Sensing the World

The lesson opens with Atlas, a robot that places boards, grabs bags, and does backflips. It can look like it senses the world the way a human does.

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px auto;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/65c0acc9040efd2355b10cac6cf8e051-image-1795018632516.png" alt="Atlas, a humanoid robot, balancing on scaffolding while handling a wooden board" style="width: 55%; border-radius: 10px;">
  <p><em>Slide 3, the "Atlas Gets a Grip" still</em></p>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <p>&#128161; <strong>Teacher tip:</strong> when you ask what senses Atlas would need, strong answers connect a specific task, placing the board, grabbing the bag, the backflip, to a specific sense: sight, touch, balance. If students stall, narrow the question: "What senses would help you do what Atlas just did?"</p>
</div>

## How Robots Sense the World

<img src="https://images.code.org/a1a564b9f61992ff3ca22ceb6c455031-image-1795018800388.png" alt="a black box with a 1 and a white box with a 0 represting bits" width="350px" style="border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 0 40px 40px;">

A computer has no eyes, no ears, only numbers, specifically, ones and zeros called **bits**. When a computer "sees" an image, it isn't looking at a picture the way a person does, it's reading a grid of those bits, where each one stands for whether a pixel is light or dark. 

That gap between what looks like a picture to us and what's actually just numbers to a computer is exactly what your students experience hands-on in the next level, filling in a grid of bits themselves and watching an image appear.

This is what a **representation** is: a way of describing something real, a picture, a sound, anything, with data a computer can understand. Every classifier your students build this unit starts with choosing a representation before a computer can sense anything at all.

